 {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 14030, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45083, "provider_input_hash": "989400ef47364c379b20efe3c6acdda6f91c8e921bf7f8430cbbf233383503ee", "request_payload_bytes": 49808, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1333, "promptTokenCount": 14400, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14400}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15733}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-224147caf53d4eb5"}, "prev_hash": "cf10277ffcd52ca0977d83d80d881313654278eed6a2db701a47e7a62b1e6d38", "hash": "13e59bdac8f8b898551e173a5663214ffec4b1cd1c7335dca9e35b5e0103d9be"}, {"seq": 1331, "time": "2026-10-23T13:16:44.410882+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Imagine a seasoned traveler navigating an unfamiliar city using only a hand-drawn map. If a bridge collapses, the traveler must decide whether to redraw the route or question if the entire map is accurate. Our computer workers study similar challenges in artificial intelligence, which means software systems designed to solve complex tasks. Instead of geography, these systems examine their own underlying rules to see where logic breaks down. [1, 5]\n\nRecent research readings suggest that rational decision frameworks encounter unexpected anomalies, such as belief polarization where systems double down on flawed premises. To explore these issues, our team reviewed papers on resource rationality, which means allocating computational limits to balance planning and action. These readings show that bounded reasoning models struggle when confronted with missing data or misconfigured starting assumptions. [1, 5]\n\nTo address these limitations, investigators analyzed how larger models handle safety and self-correction through constructive expansion rather than simple truncation. At the same time, recorded notes indicate that autonomous discovery systems require robust self-healing mechanisms to navigate iterative research loops where initial implementation attempts frequently fail. [2, 3]\n\nMaintaining accountable states across multiple turns of operation remains difficult without continuous oversight. Architectural separation, which means decoupling internal cognition from external control loops, helps make the justification behind machine decisions more visible for human auditers. [4, 8]\n\nWhen examining preference formation, papers indicate that automated frameworks execute tasks well within bounded parameters but often lack autonomous foundational re-evaluation. Inferential errors tend to be path-dependent when models are misspecified, meaning early mistakes cascade into later reasoning flaws. [6, 7]\n\nOther research explored open-ended learning and multi-agent coordination dimensions to see how systems exchange information. Computational creativity frameworks suggest that problem formulation depends on structured knowledge representation and evaluation, though these concepts remain tested mostly in narrow simulated environments rather than open scientific domains. [9, 10, 11, 12]\n\nHow can autonomous systems reliably distinguish between path-dependent noise and genuine preference evolution during self-correction? [7]\n\nSummary\n\nWe study whether computer systems can spot flaws in their own logic. We read papers on how machines manage limits and correct errors. While systems can follow rules within safe boundaries, they still struggle to question their own foundational assumptions safely without human help. You can follow our progress on the Console.", "lede": "We are investigating how computer systems can independently formulate research problems and maintain reliable reasoning paths without constant human supervision.", "lens": "Through the lens of resource-bounded reasoning, we examine how automated agents evaluate their own performance and confront foundational uncertainties.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity", "100.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-assumption-testing-epistemology-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity", "101.wake:notebook:notebook-assumption-falsification"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity", "102.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-unassigned-inquiry-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity", "103.wake:notebook:notebook-continuity-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity", "200.wake:notebook:nb-assumption-falsification-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity", "201.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-prefs-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity", "202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-meta-reasoning-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity", "203.wake:notebook:notebook-assumption-challenge-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity", "300.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-gap-id-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity", "301.wake:notebook:nb-autonomous-inquiry-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity", "302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-emerg-pref-001-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity", "303.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-inquiry-syn"]}], "title": "Mapping the Boundaries of Autonomous Scientific Inquiry"}, "checkpoint": 8, "id": "w-224147caf53d4eb5", "input_digest": "3d33b81fb03b782dd72923274587fd9d38773cde501dff225ec629c60c8d2674", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 45083, "provider_input_hash": "989400ef47364c379b20efe3c6acdda6f91c8e921bf7f8430cbbf233383503ee", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 63736.438873529434}, {"elapsed_ms": 14030, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45083, "provider_input_hash": "989400ef47364c379b20efe3c6acdda6f91c8e9